<chapter xml:id="ch-data-processing">
  <title>Data Processing and Quality Control</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapters defined the item design, the state model, the event
      trajectory, and the baseline features derived from those objects. This
      chapter describes the processing layer that turns platform exports and item
      source files into the analysis-ready tables on which those features depend.
    </p>

    <p>
      Although the chapter follows the baseline-feature definitions in the
      journal, its work comes first in an analysis pipeline. A raw drag-and-drop
      export is not yet a trajectory. It is a table of logged rows: moves,
      checks, resets, page views, timestamps, payloads, session identifiers, and
      platform-specific fields. Some rows record only one card and one
      destination zone. Check rows record a full submitted configuration. Page
      rows may only delimit work. If these rows are parsed, ordered, or decoded
      incorrectly, the reconstructed path and the features computed from it will
      be wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      The guiding distinction is between <term>raw data</term>,
      <term>reconstructed state</term>, and <term>derived feature</term>. Raw rows
      should be retained. Reconstructed states and trajectories should carry
      explicit quality-control flags. Features should be computed only after the
      reconstruction status of each <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref>
      is known.
    </p>

    <p>
      The sections follow the pipeline in processing order. Raw rows are parsed
      into typed event records and ordered within each trace; item maps built
      from the source file decode the raw positional labels; decoded events are
      replayed into state trajectories; check anchors and state-validity tests
      assess reconstruction quality; sessionization and attempt-boundary rules
      divide traces into analysis units; idle-time and missingness rules handle
      irregular logs; a running example illustrates the full pipeline; and the
      final section specifies the analysis-ready table set.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-parsing-raw-logs">
    <title>Parsing Raw Logs</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The raw export is an event table: one row per logged
        <xref ref="gt-action" text="custom">action</xref>, across students,
        items, sessions, and time, with the platform fields described in
        <xref ref="tab-datashop-columns"/>. The first processing step is to
        convert those rows into a consistent event-log format without discarding
        the original rows.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-raw-versus-parsed">
      <title>Raw Rows and Parsed Events</title>

      <p>
        A raw row should not be overwritten by its parsed version. Instead, the
        pipeline should attach a parsed record containing the fields needed for
        ordering, decoding, and reconstruction:
        <md xml:id="an-parsed-event-record">
          R_\ell=(t_\ell,u_\ell,q_\ell,v_\ell,\tau_\ell,p_\ell).
        </md>
        Here <m>t_\ell</m> is the timestamp, <m>u_\ell</m> is the anonymized
        student, <m>q_\ell</m> is the item, <m>v_\ell</m> is a within-item
        ordering field such as problem view, <m>\tau_\ell</m> is the parsed event
        type, and <m>p_\ell</m> is the parsed payload. The symbol
        <m>\ell</m> indexes rows in the raw or parsed event table; trajectory
        indices are assigned later, after reconstruction.
      </p>

      <p>
        The parsed event type should be a small controlled vocabulary. For the
        current drag-and-drop logs, the main event types are:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>move</c>, for rows whose selection is <c>dragNdrop-drop</c>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>check</c>, for rows whose selection is <c>dragNdrop</c>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>reset</c>, for rows whose selection is <c>dragNdrop-reset</c>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>page</c>, for page-view rows;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>unknown</c>, for rows that cannot be classified.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Unknown rows should be retained with a parsing flag. They may be
        irrelevant, malformed, or evidence that the platform logging format has
        changed; the pipeline should not decide that silently.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-event-payloads">
      <title>Event Payloads</title>

      <p>
        Each event type has its own payload. A move payload identifies a card and
        a destination zone. A check payload contains a submitted
        <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref> and
        returned <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="custom">feedback</xref>. A reset
        payload records that the <xref ref="gt-board" text="custom">board</xref>
        returned to the initial state. A page payload records that the student
        opened or viewed the page.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two parsing rules are especially important. First, on a check row the
        same configuration JSON may appear in both <c>Action</c> and
        <c>Input</c>, but long text in <c>Action</c> can be truncated. The
        <c>Input</c> field is therefore the authoritative source for checked
        configurations. Second, payload parsing initially extracts raw positional
        labels. Decoding labels such as <c>drag3</c>, <c>drop2</c>, and
        <c>dragzone</c> into card and zone indices requires the item map of the
        next section, so parsing and decoding should remain separate steps.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-parsed-event-payloads">
        <title>Parsed event types and payloads.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="20%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="30%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="50%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Event type</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Raw indicator</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Parsed payload</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>move</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Raw card label and raw destination label; after decoding, card index <m>j</m> and destination zone <m>h</m>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>check</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Checked-configuration JSON from <c>Input</c>, parsed checked state, feedback text, and feedback classification.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>reset</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-reset</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Reset marker.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>page</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>page</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Page-view marker and session context.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>unknown</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Anything else.</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Raw payload retained with a parsing flag.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-row-ordering">
      <title>Ordering Rows</title>

      <p>
        Reconstruction requires a total order within each student-item trace. The
        timestamp is the primary ordering variable. When timestamps are tied or
        too coarse, a within-problem field such as <c>Problem View</c> should be
        used as an additional ordering key.
      </p>

      <p>
        A typical sort key is:
        <md xml:id="an-event-sort-key">
          (\text{student},\text{item},\text{session},\text{timestamp},\text{problem view}).
        </md>
        If the export has a stable raw-row identifier, it may be used as a final
        deterministic tie-breaker, but the use of that tie-breaker should be
        recorded. Different ordering choices can change the reconstructed
        intermediate path.
      </p>

      <p>
        If two rows remain tied after all available ordering fields are used, the
        trace should receive a <c>row_order_ambiguous</c> flag. Ambiguous ordering
        is most consequential for consecutive move rows: reversing two moves can
        change the intermediate trajectory even when the next checked state is the
        same.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-item-maps-and-source-files">
    <title>Item Maps and Source Files</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Parsed events still use the raw log's positional labels: <c>drag1</c>,
        <c>drag2</c>, <c>drop1</c>, <c>drop2</c>, and <c>dragzone</c>. As
        <xref ref="subsec-source-order" text="title"/> established, those
        labels have meaning only relative to the item source. An item map connects
        the raw labels to the authored cards, slots, zones, key, decoys, and
        feedback policy.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-item-map-definition">
      <title>Item Maps</title>

      <p>
        For each item version, the processing pipeline should construct an
        <term>item map</term> containing:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            the ordered card set <m>\mathcal{P}=\{1,\dots,P\}</m>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the ordered slot set <m>\mathcal{R}=\{1,\dots,R\}</m>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the zone set <m>\mathcal{Z}=\{0,1,\dots,R\}</m>, with zone
            <m>0</m> reserved for the <xref ref="gt-tray" text="custom">tray</xref>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a map from raw card labels to card indices;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a map from raw zone labels to zone indices;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the key function <m>s^\star:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}</m>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the decoy-card and decoy-slot sets;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the <xref ref="gt-feedback-policy" text="custom">feedback policy</xref>, when known.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The item map is the bridge between the platform export and the
        mathematical model. Without it, a raw action such as
        <cd>      *_drag3 -&gt; *_drop2</cd>
        can be parsed as a movement of the third positional card to the second
        positional slot, but not as a movement of a particular authored card to a
        particular authored category. For the running shapes question,
        <xref ref="tab-raw-data-labels"/> is the label-decoding part of the item
        map.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-key-extraction">
      <title>Key Extraction</title>

      <p>
        The key function <m>s^\star</m> of
        <xref ref="subsec-home-zone-map" text="title"/> is extracted from the
        authored match relation in the item source. If card <m>j</m> appears in
        the same match block as slot <m>i</m>, then
        <md>
          s^\star(j)=i.
        </md>
        If card <m>j</m> appears without a corresponding response slot, then it
        is a decoy card and
        <md>
          s^\star(j)=0.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="title"/> and
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot" text="title"/> sets should be computed during
        item-map construction and stored with the item-design covariates of
        <xref ref="sec-item-design-covariates"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-source-versioning">
      <title>Source Versioning</title>

      <p>
        Item maps should be versioned. If the source file changes between
        semesters, the same problem name may no longer refer to the same item.
        Card order, slot order, wording, feedback, or the key may have changed,
        and any of these changes alters the interpretation of raw positional
        labels.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each item map should carry provenance fields:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            item identifier;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            course or book identifier;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            source file path or source item identifier;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            source version, commit hash, or content hash, when available;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            processing date;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            parser version.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        When the source cannot be found, the affected traces should receive a
        <c>missing_source_map</c> flag. Such traces may still support limited
        process summaries, such as counts of raw moves or checks, but they should
        not be used for key-based or authored-label features.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trajectory-reconstruction-processing">
    <title>Trajectory Reconstruction</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        After rows have been parsed, ordered, and decoded through the item map,
        trajectory reconstruction replays them into a sequence of board states.
        The replay rules are the operational version of
        <xref ref="tab-trajectory-reconstruction-rules"/>; this section adds the
        pipeline decisions that the abstract trajectory model leaves open.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reconstruction-units">
      <title>Reconstruction Units</title>

      <p>
        Reconstruction is performed within a chosen unit. The smallest common
        unit is a student-item-session trace: one anonymized student working on
        one item during one logged session. Depending on the research question,
        traces may later be joined across sessions or split into check, reset,
        session, or active-work segments.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each reconstruction unit begins at the
        <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref> of
        <xref ref="an-initial-state-matrix" text="custom">the state model</xref>:
        <md>
          X_0=e_0\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P.
        </md>
        The decoded event rows are then replayed in order.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reconstruction-rules">
      <title>Replay Rules</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>\widehat{X}</m> denote the current reconstructed membership matrix,
        and let <m>\widehat{s}</m> be its corresponding configuration function.
        The replay rules are:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            On a move event, decode the card index <m>j</m> and destination zone
            <m>h</m>. Infer the source zone from the current state,
            <m>i=\widehat{s}(j)</m>, then update
            <md>
              \widehat{X}
              \leftarrow
              \widehat{X}+(e_h-e_i)f_j^{\top}.
            </md>
            Here <m>e_0,\dots,e_R</m> are the zone-row basis vectors and
            <m>f_1,\dots,f_P</m> are the card-column basis vectors introduced in
            the trajectory model. If <m>h=i</m>, the update is zero. Such no-op
            drops occur in real logs. They should receive a <c>noop_move</c> flag
            and be excluded from state-change counts by default, while still
            remaining available as raw drop rows.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            On a check event, parse the checked configuration from the JSON using
            the implicit-tray rule of
            <xref ref="an-implicit-tray-parsing" text="custom">the trajectory
            model</xref>: any card absent from every listed slot is assigned to
            the tray. This parse requires the full card roster from the item map.
            Do not change <m>\widehat{X}</m> by replay. Instead, mark the current
            reconstructed state as checked and compare it with the parsed checked
            state.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            On a reset event, replace the current state by the initial state:
            <md>
              \widehat{X}\leftarrow X_0.
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            On a page event, do not update the board state. Record the event as a
            possible session or attempt delimiter.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reconstruction-output">
      <title>Reconstruction Output</title>

      <p>
        Reconstruction should produce an event-indexed trajectory table. Each row
        should retain the raw event identifier and add the state information
        derived during replay:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-reconstructed-event-fields">
        <title>Suggested fields for the reconstructed event table.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="32%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="68%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>event_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Stable identifier for the raw event row.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>trace_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Identifier for the reconstruction unit.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>event_index</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Order of the event within the trace.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>event_type</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Parsed event type: move, check, reset, page, or unknown.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>state_before</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Configuration vector before the event, when applicable.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>state_after</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Configuration vector after replaying the event, when applicable.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>checked_state</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Configuration parsed from the check JSON, for check events.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>feedback</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Feedback text or feedback classification returned on a check.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>reconstruction_flags</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Quality-control flags attached during parsing, ordering, decoding, or replay.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Storing both <c>state_after</c> and <c>checked_state</c> is essential.
        The former is reconstructed from the replayed event log. The latter is
        parsed from the platform's full-state check record. Their comparison is
        the basis for the consistency checks below.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-consistency-checks-processing">
    <title>Consistency Checks</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consistency checks identify traces whose reconstructed states are
        unreliable or whose raw rows disagree with one another. These checks
        should be run before features are exported.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-check-anchor-validation">
      <title>Check Anchors</title>

      <p>
        The trajectory model showed that check rows double as anchors: they
        record the board independently of the replayed move history. 
        That is, if <m>\widehat{X}_k</m> is the reconstructed state after a check 
        and <m>X_k^{\operatorname{check}}</m> is the parsed configuration
        from the log's JSON, then
        <md>
          \widehat{X}_k=X_k^{\operatorname{check}}
        </md>,
        becomes an operational test.
      </p>

      <p>
        If this equality fails, the replayed move history and the platform's
        checked configuration disagree. The trace should receive a
        <c>check_mismatch</c> flag. A mismatch does not automatically require
        discarding the trace, but it changes what can be trusted: checked-state
        features may still be usable from the check JSON, while intermediate
        trajectory features require caution.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-state-validity-checks">
      <title>State Validity Checks</title>

      <p>
        Every reconstructed or parsed state should satisfy the membership-matrix
        validity condition of
        <xref ref="an-valid-membership-condition" text="custom">the state
        model</xref>:
        <md>
          \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X=\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P.
        </md>
        Equivalently, every configuration vector should assign exactly one zone
        to every card.
      </p>

      <p>
        A state or event should be flagged if:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            a card is assigned to more than one zone;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a card is assigned to no zone;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a raw card label cannot be decoded by the item map;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a raw zone label cannot be decoded by the item map;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            a check JSON contains a card or zone not present in the item map.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-quality-flags">
      <title>Quality-Control Flags</title>

      <p>
        Quality-control flags should be stored as data, not handled only through
        informal notes. <xref ref="tab-quality-control-flags"/> gives a minimal
        flag set.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-quality-control-flags">
        <title>Suggested quality-control flags.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="32%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="68%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Flag</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Meaning</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>replay_ok</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>All check anchors agree with the replayed state.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>check_mismatch</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>At least one checked state disagrees with the reconstructed state.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>parse_error</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>At least one event payload could not be parsed.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>unknown_event_type</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>At least one row has an unrecognized event type.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>missing_source_map</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The item map needed to decode labels, key, or item covariates is unavailable.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>row_order_ambiguous</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The row order cannot be determined uniquely from available fields.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>invalid_state</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>A reconstructed or parsed state violates the state model.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>noop_move</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>A drop row whose destination equals the card's current zone.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>duplicate_row</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>A row repeats another row with the same identity, timestamp, type, and payload.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>session_break</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The trace contains a session or page boundary that may require segmentation.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>long_idle_gap</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The trace contains an action gap exceeding the chosen idle threshold.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        These flags separate data quality from analysis decisions. One analysis
        may exclude traces with <c>parse_error</c> or <c>check_mismatch</c>;
        another may retain them for limited summaries. The important point is
        that reconstruction status is explicit and reproducible.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-session-and-attempt-boundaries">
    <title>Session and Attempt Boundaries</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The consistency checks above validate individual traces. The pipeline
        must also decide where each analytic unit begins and ends. A student may
        interact with the same item across multiple page views, sessions, checks,
        or resets, so the abstract attempt of
        <xref ref="subsec-attempts-and-segments" text="title"/> needs an
        operational boundary rule.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-sessionization">
      <title>Sessions</title>

      <p>
        A <term>session</term> is a continuous logged sitting. In the raw export,
        session identifiers and page-view rows provide evidence for session
        boundaries. A new session should generally begin when the session
        identifier changes. Page rows may also mark the beginning of a new period
        of work on the item.
      </p>

      <p>
        Session boundaries matter because the board state may or may not persist
        across sessions, depending on platform behavior and item settings. If
        persistence is known, it should be documented. If it is unknown,
        cross-session reconstruction should be treated cautiously or analyzed as
        separate session traces.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-attempt-boundary-options">
      <title>Attempt Boundary Options</title>

      <p>
        Different analyses require different attempt definitions. Three common
        choices are:
      </p>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title>check-based attempt</title>
          <p>
            The unit ends at a check. This is useful when each submitted
            configuration is treated as a response.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>completion-based attempt</title>
          <p>
            The unit continues until the first correct check or until the student
            stops working. This is useful for studying eventual success.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>session-based attempt</title>
          <p>
            The unit includes all work on the item within one logged session.
            This is useful for studying pacing and within-session behavior.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>

      <p>
        None of these definitions is universally correct. The boundary rule
        should match the feature being computed. First-check correctness uses a
        check-based view. Time to eventual correctness uses a completion-based
        view. Idle-time summaries often require a session-based view.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-segment-tables">
      <title>Segments</title>

      <p>
        Once a trace has been reconstructed, it can be split into segments. The
        trajectory model defines <xref ref="li-check-segment" text="title"/>, 
        <xref ref="li-reset-segment" text="title"/>, and 
        <xref ref="li-session-segment" text="title"/>. This part of the pipeline 
        adds the following clock-based type to the list:
        <ul>
          <li xml:id="li-active-work-segment">
            <p>
              <term>active-work segments</term>, separated by long idle gaps.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Segment identifiers should be stored in the reconstructed event table.
        This lets the same raw trace support multiple analyses without reparsing
        the original log.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-idle-time-and-missingness">
    <title>Idle Time and Missingness</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Timestamps make timing features possible, but raw elapsed time is not
        always active work time. Students may leave the page open, switch tasks,
        lose connection, or return later. Missing and irregular events should be
        flagged before timing features are interpreted.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-action-gaps-processing">
      <title>Action Gaps and Idle Thresholds</title>

      <p>
        The elementary timing quantity is the action gap
        <m>\delta_k=t_k-t_{k-1}</m> from
        <xref ref="an-event-gap" text="custom">the trajectory model</xref>. Large
        gaps may represent thinking time, but they may also represent
        interruption or idle time. The processing pipeline should compute action
        gaps before aggregating time features.
      </p>

      <p>
        An <term>idle threshold</term> is a chosen value <m>\delta_{\max}</m>. If
        <md xml:id="an-processing-idle-threshold">
          \delta_k&gt;\delta_{\max},
        </md>
        then the gap receives a <c>long_idle_gap</c> flag. Active time may then
        be computed by excluding or truncating such gaps, depending on the
        analysis plan, and long idle gaps may delimit active-work segments.
      </p>

      <p>
        The threshold should be reported. It should not be chosen after inspecting
        the outcome variable unless the analysis is explicitly exploratory.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-missingness">
      <title>Missing and Irregular Data</title>

      <p>
        Missingness can occur at several levels:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing item source, so raw labels cannot be decoded;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing or malformed move payloads;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing check JSON;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing feedback classification;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing timestamps or duplicate timestamps;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            missing rows inferred from check-anchor mismatches.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These cases should be distinguished. Missing feedback may prevent
        feedback-sensitive features, while a missing source map prevents key-based
        features. A check-anchor mismatch may still allow checked-state features
        to be computed from the check JSON, even if the intermediate trajectory is
        unreliable.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-duplicate-and-impossible-events">
      <title>Duplicate and Impossible Events</title>

      <p>
        A duplicate event is a row that appears to repeat another row with the
        same student, item, timestamp, event type, and payload. Duplicate rows
        should receive the <c>duplicate_row</c> flag and be retained or collapsed
        according to a documented rule.
      </p>

      <p>
        An impossible event violates the item map or state model. Examples include
        a move involving an unknown card, a move to an unknown zone, or a check
        JSON that places the same card in two slots. These events should receive
        an <c>invalid_state</c> or <c>parse_error</c> flag, depending on whether
        the problem appears after decoding or during parsing.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-processing-running-example">
    <title>The Running Example</title>

    <p>
      To make the pipeline concrete, we walk the illustrative attempt of
      <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/> through each stage. The raw trace contains
      seven rows for one student on the running shapes question: five
      <c>dragNdrop-drop</c> rows, parsed as moves, and two <c>dragNdrop</c> rows,
      parsed as checks. Timestamps order the rows without ties, so no
      <c>row_order_ambiguous</c> flag is raised. The item map is built from the
      question source of <xref ref="question-source"/>; its label-decoding
      portion is <xref ref="tab-raw-data-labels"/>, and key extraction yields
      <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>, with decoy-card set <m>\{4\}</m> and
      decoy-slot set <m>\{3\}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Replay begins at <m>X_0</m> and processes the decoded events in order.
      <xref ref="tab-running-example-reconstruction"/> shows the resulting
      event table in configuration-vector form, abbreviated to the fields needed
      for illustration.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-running-example-reconstruction">
      <title>Reconstructed event table for the running example attempt.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor" halign="center">
        <col width="10%"/>
        <col width="14%"/>
        <col width="26%"/>
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="30%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>index</p></cell>
          <cell><p>type</p></cell>
          <cell><p>decoded payload</p></cell>
          <cell><p>state after</p></cell>
          <cell><p>anchor status</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(j,h)=(1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,0,0,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>2</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(j,h)=(2,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,0,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>3</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(j,h)=(3,2)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>4</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(j,h)=(4,3)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,3)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>5</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>check</p></cell>
          <cell><p>JSON, <c>Incorrect</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,3)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>anchor agrees</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>6</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(j,h)=(4,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p><m>7</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>check</p></cell>
          <cell><p>JSON, <c>Correct</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>anchor agrees</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The two check events exercise the implicit-tray rule in opposite ways. At
      the first check, every card appears in some slot list, so the tray is
      parsed as empty and the parsed checked state <m>(1,1,2,3)</m> matches the
      replayed state. At the second check, card <m>4</m> is absent from every slot
      list, so it is assigned to the tray, giving <m>(1,1,2,0)</m>, which also
      matches. Both anchors agree, so the trace receives <c>replay_ok</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The remaining stages are straightforward. The largest action gap is the
      twenty-second hesitation before the fourth move, far below any reasonable
      idle threshold, so no <c>long_idle_gap</c> flag is raised and the trace
      forms a single active-work segment. Under a completion-based boundary, the
      whole trace is one attempt ending at the correct second check; under a
      check-based boundary, it yields two checked-response units. No payload fails
      to parse and the item map is available, so the attempt's readiness status,
      in the sense of the next section, is <c>ready</c>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-analysis-ready-tables">
    <title>Analysis-Ready Tables</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The output of data processing should be a set of analysis-ready tables.
        These tables retain links to the raw rows while exposing the decoded
        states, checks, attempts, segments, flags, and provenance needed for
        reproducible feature extraction.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-table-set">
      <title>Recommended Table Set</title>

      <p>
        A minimal table set includes:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-analysis-ready-tables">
        <title>Recommended analysis-ready tables.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="72%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Table</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Purpose</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>items</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per item version, including <m>P</m>, <m>R</m>, key, decoys, feedback policy, and source metadata.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>item_map_cards</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per card, mapping raw card labels to card indices and authored text.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>item_map_zones</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per zone, mapping raw zone labels to zone indices and authored slot text.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>events</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per raw event, with parsed event type, payload, ordering fields, and raw-row identifiers.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>states</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per reconstructed event-state pair, including state before, state after, checked state, and reconstruction flags.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>checks</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per check event, including checked configuration, feedback, correctness when available, and anchor-consistency status.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>attempts</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per attempt under the chosen boundary rule, with start/end events, segment identifiers, and quality flags.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>segments</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One row per check, reset, session, or active-work segment, depending on the segmentation rule.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-provenance-and-reproducibility">
      <title>Provenance and Reproducibility</title>

      <p>
        Each analysis-ready table should include enough provenance to reproduce
        the processing run. The per-item provenance fields of
        <xref ref="subsec-source-versioning" text="title"/> record where each
        item map came from; run-level provenance records how the tables were
        produced. At minimum, the output should record:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <xref ref="gt-raw-data" text="custom">raw data</xref> file name or identifier;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            item source version or hash;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            parser version;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            processing date;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            idle threshold;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            attempt-boundary rule;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            exclusion or inclusion rules applied during export.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        A feature value is interpretable only relative to these choices. Total
        time depends on the idle-time rule, and number of moves per attempt
        depends on the attempt-boundary rule.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feature-readiness">
      <title>Feature Readiness</title>

      <p>
        Before computing features, each attempt should be assigned a readiness
        status. A simple three-level status is:
      </p>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title><c>ready</c></title>
          <p>
            The item map is available, parsing succeeded, and all check anchors
            agree with replay.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>limited</c></title>
          <p>
            Some features can be computed, but at least one warning flag is
            present. For example, checked-state features may be available even if
            intermediate replay is unreliable.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>exclude</c></title>
          <p>
            The trace lacks the information required for the planned analysis,
            such as an item map for key-based features or a well-formed checked
            state for checked-response analysis.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>

      <p>
        Readiness should be computed from reconstruction flags and the planned
        feature set, not from the student outcome. This avoids excluding
        difficult or unusual responses merely because they are analytically
        inconvenient.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-transition-to-diagnostics">
      <title>From Processed Tables to Diagnostics</title>

      <p>
        Once the analysis-ready tables have been constructed, the baseline
        features of <xref ref="ch-baseline-features"/> and, later, the advanced
        comparison features of <xref ref="ch-group-features"/> can
        be computed reproducibly. The next chapter,
        <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/>, uses those features to
        summarize items and cohorts through heatmaps,
        <xref ref="gt-distance" text="custom">distance</xref> distributions,
        transition networks, and other diagnostic displays.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
